    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "IE:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " draw ≥ 2 (ideally 3) blood culture sets before any antibiotic. Empiric (seriously ill), typical US regimen: vancomycin + ceftriaxone; adjust to culture results as soon as possible."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Native valve, no history of penicillin anaphylaxis: penicillin-susceptible streptococci, ceftriaxone 2 g IV/IM daily × 4 wk; oxacillin-susceptible staphylococci, cefazolin 2 g IV q8h × 6 wk. HACEK: ceftriaxone 2 g daily × 4 wk."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Hypersensitivity (most common; rash common, anaphylaxis rare), C. difficile diarrhea, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, Coombs+ (hemolytic anemia very uncommon). Ceftriaxone: immune hemolytic anemia (stop if anemia develops); ceftriaxone-calcium precipitates in gallbladder (pseudolithiasis, may mimic gallstones) and urinary tract."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Prior major hypersensitivity to a cephalosporin. Cross-reactivity with penicillin is about 2%, negligible for third- or fourth-generation agents or dissimilar side chains; may be given cautiously after delayed penicillin hypersensitivity if necessary. Ceftriaxone: contraindicated in hyperbilirubinemic preterm neonates and in neonates ≤28 days needing IV calcium."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Ceftriaxone + IV calcium:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " never simultaneously or in the same line (precipitates); beyond the neonatal period, may be given sequentially if the line is flushed."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
